Title: What They Called Recovery
Credit: Animated feature screenplay — scene draft
Author: Christopher Kokkinos
Draft date: 2026-09-28
Status: SC014 proposed for individual author scene review; combined pass provisionally accepted

INT. CONTROLLED HOUSE - FRIDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

An older WOMAN brings a paper cup before the showing. Zoe remembers the warning, raises it, holds the bitter liquid behind her teeth while the woman watches, then spits it into a sink and rinses. She cannot know whether she swallowed some. When adults return with her cleaned school uniform, Zoe moves slowly and lets her focus drift. She notices their keys and corridor checks while they treat her as sedated.

INT. SHOWING ROOM - FRIDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

Bright panels, a square of pale tape and dark glass. Zoe is placed on the square. Voices from behind the glass ask about original and placement documentation, medication and travel. A light crosses her teeth. She fixes her eyes on a chip at the bottom of the glass while the room tips slightly.

VOICE (O.S.)
This number doesn't match the transfer sheet.

Another voice insists the original record holds the first identifier. A dispute over access and payment rises beyond the glass. A door opens; the woman leaves. The speaker dies. Zoe remains on the taped square through several breaths, then chooses to step out. She catches the frame when her footing fails.

INT. CONTROLLED HOUSE - ARCHIVE/SERVICE PASSAGE - FRIDAY - ZOE'S ACCOUNT

The archive door and safe stand open. Numbered folders, a leather ledger, hard drives and a laptop crowd the room. On the desk: the childhood photograph and the number Zoe saw on Wednesday. A clear property bag holds her school things and backpack. She takes her folder and belongings. A small black drive is connected to the laptop beside her record. She cannot read the screen, but pulls the drive free and packs it too. The laptop chimes. She hides as an injured man's escort passes the doorway.

In a service passage two men move an injured person through a door held open by a crate. Their attention is elsewhere. Zoe slips outside; her backpack catches briefly and she frees it. She runs toward traffic, hides behind bins until she can count ten breaths, and finds a bus stop with a town-centre destination she recognises.

A DRIVER sees her school uniform and waves her aboard despite her having no fare. At the interchange she changes to a familiar route, keeps her bag close and passes the school stop. She stays aboard for home. At her own gate she hesitates, then knocks.

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - PRESENT DAY

ELIAS
You knew the folder was yours?

ZOE
I knew the photograph and number. I didn't understand the rest until the night before the flight. I still haven't properly opened the drive.

ELIAS
Why take it?

ZOE
They cared enough to fight over the archive. I thought it might tell me who I was.

He looks at her in the mirror. She still holds the backpack.

ELIAS
You were frightened. You still chose to take your file and get out. Both can be true.

Her grip loosens a little.

ZOE
There's more.

ELIAS
Take your time.
